Yes. You do not actually need to resolve “was this engineered?” before deciding whether the environment is bad for the people inside it.

A useful split is:

\text{intentional design}

\text{incentive-driven emergence}

\text{old tactic reappearing in a new medium}

\text{ordinary adaptation to a changed environment}

Those can produce very similar surface behavior. Algorithms, advertisers, creators, peer groups, parents, and kids can all adapt to one another until the system looks coordinated even when no single actor designed the whole thing.

That is why the better question is often not “who planned this?” but:

What incentives are shaping the environment, what behaviors are they selecting for, and what happens if I stop participating in the loop?

For parents especially, stepping away can be an experiment rather than a moral statement. Remove or reduce one input for a week—short-form feeds, notifications, background screens, whatever—and observe sleep, attention, irritability, boredom tolerance, conversation, play, and family rhythm. Then you have a delta instead of a theory.

\text{baseline}
\rightarrow
\text{remove variable}
\rightarrow
\text{observe change}
\rightarrow
\text{decide}

And “old tactics” absolutely can become useful again when environments recur. Device-free meals, boredom, outdoor wandering, books, chores, unstructured play, delayed gratification, family rituals—none are magical because they are old. They matter when they counterbalance whatever the current environment is overproducing.

So the strategic move is not necessarily to prove the environment is “cheated.” It is to preserve enough distance that you can still see the game board, test the rules, and choose which games your family actually wants to play.